Task 8: What is the full path to the binary on this machine has special capabilities that can be abused to obtain root privileges?
  This was a bit challenging (not really) due to not knowing which tools to use (I wasn't familiar with them). I utilized getcap in order to figure out which binary fit.
    getcap -r / 2>/dev/null
  I used "2>/dev/null" just in case I needed to ignore the permissions denied output (which is a lot when working with the root folder and using recursive flags). The output
  generated showed aproximately 4 binaries along with their capabilities. I selected the one that set user IDs because if it has that capability, anyone can set themselves to root.

Task 9: Submit the flag located in root's home directory.
  I solved this one by running the script located in /usr/bin/ which was ./python3.8 which dropped me into a python interpreter. Once inside the interpreter, I imported os, verified
  it worked by displaying the current user ID, set the user ID to root, and then dropped into a shell as root. 
    ./python3.8
      import os
        os.getuid()
          os.setuid(0)
            os.system("/bin/bash")
  Once there I was able to navigate to the root's home directory and find the flag
    whoami
      cd root
        cat <filename>.txt
